<h3>1. Start every project with <code>/init</code></h3>
<p>When you bring Claude Code into a project that already has code, the first command is <code>/init</code>. Claude reads the codebase and writes a <code>CLAUDE.md</code> with build commands, test instructions, and project conventions — this file is a project guide that gets read automatically at the start of every session, so you stop re-explaining the project each time. If a <code>CLAUDE.md</code> already exists, it suggests improvements instead of overwriting.</p>
<p class="scn-sub">Official docs: <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/memory" target="_blank" rel="noopener">How Claude remembers your project</a> — this wiki's <a href="#!ccb-claudemd">CLAUDE.md hands-on</a> and <a href="#!claude-md-commandments">CLAUDE.md commandments</a> cover the same ground.</p>

<h3>2. Turn on the status line</h3>
<p>If you work in the terminal, set up a status bar with <code>/statusline</code>. The model in use, the git branch, and — most importantly — <strong>the remaining context percentage</strong> stay visible under the input box. Once you can see how full the context is, habits 3 through 5 below come naturally. The desktop app already shows this, so no setup needed there.</p>
<p class="scn-sub">Official docs: <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/statusline" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Status line configuration</a> — the displayed items are fully scriptable.</p>

<h3>3. Keep the context small</h3>
<p>A command like "review the entire codebase" burns tens of percent of your context in one go. Have Claude <strong>split the problem first</strong> — not "fix the payment system" but "break the payment system fix into small steps," then run each step separately. However good the model is, it forgets earlier conversation once context runs short.</p>

<h3>4. Find the token hogs with <code>/context</code></h3>
<p>If your context fills too fast, type <code>/context</code>. It visualizes which files, tools, and messages are taking how much of the window. The culprit is usually one of three things: a large file read whole, an MCP server that loaded its full tool definitions (tip 22 in the advanced part), or a long conversation history.</p>
<p class="scn-sub">Official docs: <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/commands" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Slash commands reference</a></p>

<h3>5. Around 60%, run <code>/compact</code>; between tasks, run <code>/clear</code></h3>
<p>The difference matters. <code>/compact</code> <strong>summarizes and compresses</strong> the conversation history — you can say what to keep ("keep the decisions"), and the thread continues. <code>/clear</code> is a <strong>full reset</strong>, the same as opening a new conversation. When one task ends and another begins, resetting beats compressing — leftovers from the previous task cannot contaminate the new one.</p>

