<h3>6. Start in plan mode</h3>
<p>Plan mode touches no files — it only <strong>reads, analyzes, and plans</strong> (switch with <code>Shift+Tab</code> in the terminal). Since nothing changes, you can hand over anything with peace of mind, and while planning Claude asks you questions that narrow down your intent. The harder your request is to spell out, the more plan mode is worth.</p>
<p class="scn-sub">One piece of background — since 14 August 2026, new sessions default to <strong>auto mode</strong>, which no longer asks approval at every step (<a href="#!news-20260814-auto-mode-stateless-mcp">Defaults That Stop Asking</a>). That makes "plan before touching anything" more important, not less — execution got faster, so agree on the direction first. Mode details: <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/permissions" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Permissions and modes</a></p>

<h3>7. Treat it like a junior developer</h3>
<p>If you say "write a function that adjusts padding by screen width," you have already done the hardest work yourself — defining the problem and choosing the approach. Give it the problem instead: <strong>"the video gets cut off on some devices — how should we fix this?"</strong> Claude can read the whole project and propose an approach, so ideating together and letting it execute its own plan uses the tool at full size.</p>

<h3>8. Make Claude ask you back</h3>
<p>When your requirement is fuzzy, end the prompt with: <strong>"keep asking me questions until you understand what I want at 95%."</strong> Claude fires off clarifying questions, and once that exchange ends it heads in the right direction from the start. Answering five questions is cheaper than redoing vague instructions three times.</p>

<h3>9. Plant self-checks into the to-dos</h3>
<p>When you hand over a task list, spell out the checks at the end — "open Chrome DevTools and check console errors," "do not move on until everything passes," and above all <strong>"do not move to the next to-do until you are 95%+ confident the current one is complete and correct."</strong> Without self-checks, ten to-dos each finish at 60% completeness, the gaps pile up, and later you burn tokens hunting causes you can no longer find. Plant the checks and the first deliverable comes out at a different level.</p>

